---
/**
 * /provenance/ — the carried effect log, off the landing page.
 *
 * Copied from `components/home/Provenance.astro`, which stays on the landing
 * page until cleanup retires it.
 *
 * The headline excerpt is upgraded here. The corridor block the landing page
 * shows carries three domains; the retropulsion block carries five in one
 * uninterrupted stream — flow regime with its Knudsen number and the
 * supersonic-to-subsonic cascade, the navigation filter, a control commit that
 * reads the filter's own sigma, two fork re-seeds naming their world, the
 * solver's carrier rebuilding itself mid-run, and the aero closure declaring
 * itself inert. Both blocks are quoted verbatim from committed run output.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { EXAMPLES_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Provenance — DeepCausality CFD';
const description =
  'One append-only effect log continues across regime changes, across physics domains, and across a counterfactual fan-out, so the same quantity can be read on both sides of a transition.';

/** retropulsion/output.txt:55-68, verbatim. */
const retroLog = `regime -> continuum (GNSS-denied), Kn=0.009825077357757745, supersonic / thrust-unknown
regime -> continuum (GNSS-available), Kn=0.00023029462780754038, supersonic / thrust-unknown
nav: aided (position fix folded)
ignition corridor committed at step 2117: Mach 1.997501570461523, q 2229.28 Pa, nav aided, sigma 0.38127861761973975 m
march paused at step 2118
leg re-seeded from step 2118 in world 'measured_day': coupled field carried, marched fluid state re-seeded from the world seed
regime -> continuum (GNSS-available), Kn=0.000019032190686017852, supersonic / burn
regime -> continuum (GNSS-available), Kn=0.000007544601182537176, transonic / burn
regime -> continuum (GNSS-available), Kn=0.0000031835841432511187, subsonic / burn
march paused at step 731
leg re-seeded from step 731 in world 'terminal_descent': coupled field carried, marched fluid state re-seeded from the world seed
carrier rebuilt at step 1: s_ref 1.4 -> 2.7683613571273638 (rebuild 1)
SRP drag closure inert: no throttle commanded, no decrement applied
stopping burn started at step 2445: altitude 139.12 m, speed 48.99 m/s, thrust-to-weight 2.874449170180377`;

/** corridor/output.txt:56-62, verbatim. The blackout window. */
const blackoutLog = `regime -> slip (GNSS-available), Kn=0.07829109848665225
nav: aided (position fix folded)
regime -> slip (GNSS-denied), Kn=0.012690837165407727
nav: dead reckoning (no usable fix)
march paused at step 131
leg re-seeded from step 131 in world 'fine_bank_08': coupled field carried, ...
!!ContextAlternation!!: world 'nominal_descent' replaced with 'fine_bank_08'`;

/** Read straight off the corridor's per-leg summary lines. */
const legs = [
  { leg: 'Onset, 73.2 km', link: 'GNSS denied', err: '0.1823 m', var: '2.290e-1 m²' },
  { leg: 'Peak passage, 60.9 km', link: 'GNSS denied', err: '1.5637 m', var: '2.671e1 m²' },
  { leg: 'Flow-resolved exit, 46.8 km', link: 'GNSS available', err: '2.2879 m', var: '2.999e0 m²' },
  { leg: 'Reacquisition, 47.4 km', link: 'GNSS available', err: '0.2804 m', var: '3.184e-1 m²' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/provenance/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <article class="page vpage">
    <header class="vhead">
      <p class="eyebrow">Provenance</p>
      <h1>One log, across every boundary</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The append-only effect log continues across regimes, across physics
        domains, and across a counterfactual fan-out. That is what lets the same
        quantity be read on both sides of a transition.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="vgroup">
      <h2>What gets an entry</h2>
      <p class="body">
        Every stage that changes the story of a run appends one: a regime
        transition, a navigation mode change, a pause, a leg re-seed, a world
        replacement, a solver rebuild, a control commit. Fourteen consecutive
        entries from the committed retropulsion descent, which carries
        twenty-two in total:
      </p>

      <Code code={retroLog} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />

      <p class="body">
        Five domains are interleaved in that one sequence. The flow regime
        carries its Knudsen number and cascades supersonic to transonic to
        subsonic. The navigation filter reports its mode. The guidance commits
        an ignition corridor and quotes the filter's own sigma as the reason.
        Two legs re-seed, each naming the world it continues in. The solver
        rebuilds its own carrier mid-run. And the aero closure declares itself
        inert rather than silently applying nothing.
      </p>

      <p class="body">
        That is what "across boundaries" means in practice: one chain to read,
        rather than four logs to correlate afterwards.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="vgroup">
      <h2>The blackout window</h2>
      <p class="body">
        The two adjacent entries in the middle of this block are the whole
        scenario. The flow said the link was gone, and the very next entry is
        the filter falling back to dead reckoning. Nothing scheduled that; the
        chemistry located it.
      </p>

      <Code code={blackoutLog} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} />

      <p class="body">
        The <code>!!ContextAlternation!!</code> entry names both sides of the
        intervention, the world replaced and the world that replaced it, so a
        counterfactual is auditable rather than implied.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="vgroup">
      <h2>Comparing across a transition</h2>
      <p class="body">
        Because the log spans the transition, the same quantity can be read on
        both sides of it. The corridor's navigation error through the blackout,
        as committed:
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Leg</th>
              <th scope="col">Link</th>
              <th scope="col">Error vs truth</th>
              <th scope="col">Position variance</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {legs.map((l) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{l.leg}</th>
                <td>{l.link}</td>
                <td class="num">{l.err}</td>
                <td class="num">{l.var}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="body">
        Variance grows by two orders of magnitude in the dark, then comes back
        once fixes resume. The retropulsion descent supports the same reading
        across a Mach boundary: it logs supersonic, transonic and subsonic
        entries in one sequence with the thrust state on each line, so the flow
        parameters before and after the transition sit in the same record.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="vgroup">
      <h2>Comparing across branches</h2>
      <p class="body">
        Each branch of a fan-out writes its own scenario log, so a variant that
        failed can be diffed against one that passed rather than re-run under a
        debugger. The weather table gates on exactly this: every dispersion
        world must carry the marker naming its baseline.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        Where a campaign uses the coupled <code>march_for</code> path, an
        optional <code>save_log(path)</code> flushes provenance to disk: one
        file per branch, plus a <code>.main.log</code> naming every spawn and
        rejoin. The <code>fork</code> / <code>branch</code> /
        <code>continue_for</code> chain does not thread that sink and writes no
        files; those branches still carry their full provenance in the returned
        report.
      </p>

      <ul class="nums">
        <li>Corridor descent: 16 entries covering four legs, two regime bands and one committed counterfactual.</li>
        <li>Retropulsion descent: 22 entries covering blackout, ignition commit, a carrier rebuild, the stopping burn and touchdown.</li>
        <li>Weather table: 48 descents, each carrying its own log, gated on the alternation marker naming its baseline.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="vgroup">
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/tutorial/stage-2-weather/">Walk 2: one baseline, six worlds</a>
        <a href="/blueprints/fork-a-running-simulation/">Blueprint: fork a running simulation</a>
        <a href={EXAMPLES_URL}>The committed run output <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .vpage { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .vhead { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .vhead h1 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); }
  .vhead .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .vgroup { margin-bottom: var(--space-7); }
  .vgroup h2 {
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .body { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--space-3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-eyebrow);
    letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-weight: 400;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; }
  tbody td { color: var(--fg-1); }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  tbody tr { transition: background-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease-out); }
  tbody tr:hover { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 5%, transparent); }

  .nums { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .nums li {
    color: var(--fg-1);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    font-size: var(--t-mono-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
  }
</style>
